 "director": {
  "mode": "hand",
  "notes": "Their claim (68:1-4) is framed («بهت المشركين»); Al-Muyassar's examples of the claim are not shown. 68:8-14 is not glossed (Al-Muyassar's «يكون له ولد» is 6:101 wording). Night and day for 67, geometric for «سبحانه هو الغني», stars for the heavens, wind for «متاع في الدنيا». STAGING v4: c1 (67), the night for rest and the day for sight: a town at nightfall (dusk, windows lit; a full night stage cannot brighten into day), a family (four faceless people, men and a woman) sitting at rest (67:6, both tafsirs: rest after the toil of seeking a living); a night-and-day plate (67:5-9); the sun rises over the town on 67:8 (the day, giving sight) and the family stands (67:9) and walks off to its affairs (67:10, As-Sa'di: they go about their livelihoods); they look up at the sky for signs for a people who listen (67:13). Not the sleep pose (it reads as shrouded bodies). c2 (68-70) stays ambient: the claim that Allah took a son (framed by the gloss, never acted by anyone), His glorification and self-sufficiency, the Prophet's ﷺ instruction (69) and the return and punishment in the Hereafter (70). Mood changed from geometric to night_sky: the verse refutes a son, and Al-Muyassar names the Messiah among the claims, so the Names' mood is not used over it. params.quiet over Allah's first-person words on the return and the punishment (70:4-13). Plates kept: geometric for glorified is He (68:5-7), stars for the heavens (68:10-14, gone before 68:22), wind for brief enjoyment (70:1-3)."
 },
